WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.459 --> 00:00:09.125
NARRATOR: It's not
really about the hunting.

00:00:09.417 --> 00:00:16.375
It's just being out
 in the woods, and seeing the
 different species of animals.

00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:19.417
Just being out there alone.

00:00:19.417 --> 00:00:20.167
The serenity.

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:26.959
Just discovering new
 things and looking at
 just how nature acts.

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:29.751
(screams)

00:00:35.709 --> 00:00:37.375
NARRATOR: Palm Beach
 County, Florida.

00:00:37.375 --> 00:00:41.083
Home to the JW Corbett
Wildlife Management area.

00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:44.083
Nestled between West
 Palm Beach and Lake
 Okeechobee,

00:00:44.083 --> 00:00:48.209
lies over 60,000 acres
 of protected forests
 and wetlands.

00:00:48.209 --> 00:00:54.667
Known as the Old Florida
 Tangle, this preserve hosts
 a diverse array of wildlife.

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:58.042
From deer and Bobcats to
water fowl and reptiles.

00:00:58.042 --> 00:01:02.167
A rich bounty that attracts
 the most passionate
 outdoor enthusiasts.

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:08.334
People like James and Terisa
 Boyce who've been hunting
 here for over 15 years.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.501
JAMES: I've been hunting
since I was five years old.

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:12.584
I started out when I was
a young fella in Maine.

00:01:12.584 --> 00:01:18.918
And my father took me in the
woods and with my brothers
learned how to hunt.

00:01:18.918 --> 00:01:22.209
And from thereon,
I moved to Florida.

00:01:22.209 --> 00:01:25.000
And I carried on
hunting to this day.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.042
TERISA: The first time I
actually went officially

00:01:28.042 --> 00:01:29.751
'hunting' James took me

00:01:29.751 --> 00:01:32.667
and you know we walked
through the woods together

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:34.834
and I got to see like
a deer in the wild.

00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:39.209
Flash forward 16 years
later, and we're still
doing the same thing.

00:01:39.209 --> 00:01:42.042
I'm still following
him around the woods.
(laughs)

00:01:42.042 --> 00:01:47.209
I've learned so much
about the animals and
what they can provide.

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:49.417
And the different
nutritional values.

00:01:49.417 --> 00:01:52.542
So it's been like the
field to table process.

00:01:52.542 --> 00:01:57.709
JAMES: We have never ate
anything from the store
in ten years for meat.

00:01:57.709 --> 00:02:00.584
We only eat off the land.

00:02:00.584 --> 00:02:01.751
NARRATOR: It's the
beginning of the season.

00:02:01.751 --> 00:02:05.125
And James and Terisa
 are hoping to get
 their first deer.

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:11.292
JAMES: That morning,
November 9th, 2019, was
like any other morning.

00:02:11.292 --> 00:02:12.083
It's first day of hunting.

00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:17.375
And was just supposed
to be a normal,
regular hunting day.

00:02:17.375 --> 00:02:19.334
TERISA: So, we arrive
at our location.

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:21.667
And then we lock the
truck up, grab our gear.

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:25.125
So, it's cloudy, it's
kind of gross looking.

00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:25.751
A little drizzle.

00:02:25.751 --> 00:02:27.751
JAMES: So, we went
by trail three.

00:02:27.751 --> 00:02:34.083
Trail three goes all the
way from the top of Corbett
down to the backside.

00:02:34.083 --> 00:02:35.834
And around. The first
day of hunting

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:38.792
is when everybody gets
in the woods to hunt.

00:02:38.792 --> 00:02:41.709
And there's so many
people, in swamp
buggies and shooting.

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:46.834
And so we knew that
buggies hunt from the
Main Road to trail three.

00:02:46.834 --> 00:02:49.459
So, what do they do? They
scare all the deer that way.

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:53.167
NARRATOR: James and Terisa
 continue down Trail three
 hoping to intercept

00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:56.834
the deer being scared
 off by other hunters
 in the area.

00:02:56.834 --> 00:03:01.000
JAMES: By that time
it was probably around
8.30, quarter to nine.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.709
We get about three miles.

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:05.667
TERISA: We're navigating
through the water

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:08.334
and the rain is starting
to rain a little harder.

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:11.751
My feet are getting soaked.
I'm getting soaked. And

00:03:11.751 --> 00:03:14.501
at this point it was
chilly. And I'm miserable.

00:03:14.501 --> 00:03:17.667
I'm just, I'm done. I'm
like let's just go back.

00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:19.250
JAMES: And I said you
know, you're right.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.250
It is started to rain a
little harder. Let's go.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.250
TERISA: So, now we're in
headed back to the car mode.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:27.375
So, you're not really
paying as much attention.

00:03:27.375 --> 00:03:29.834
And I was just trying
to keep up with him.

00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:32.751
JAMES: She was trailing
behind, ten to 15 feet.

00:03:32.751 --> 00:03:36.542
And I remember her
saying what she wanted
to eat for breakfast.

00:03:36.542 --> 00:03:39.209
And I was like yeah
that sounds good.

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:43.542
I step around a Palmetto
bush and all of a sudden
it felt like I stepped

00:03:43.542 --> 00:03:48.417
right into an electrical
outlet. Like a jolt.

00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:49.542
And all of a sudden
my leg's numb.

00:03:49.542 --> 00:03:54.959
And I like how did
electrical get out here,
three miles in the woods?

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:57.459
TERISA: And then I
heard him scream.

00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:00.459
(screams)

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:02.459
TERISA: I'm thinking
he got bit by a snake.

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:06.709
That was my first thought.
Oh my gosh, it's a snake.

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:14.667
JAMES: And I look down
and I got a gator with my
whole leg in his mouth.

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:20.334
NARRATOR: The American
 Alligator evolved over
 a 150 million years.

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:23.876
Around since the time
 of the dinosaurs.

00:04:23.876 --> 00:04:29.876
Found in fresh water rivers,
 swamps and marshes across the
 south eastern United States,

00:04:29.876 --> 00:04:34.709
this large semi-aquatic
 reptile can grow up to
 15 feet long

00:04:34.709 --> 00:04:37.626
and weigh over 1800
 pounds making them

00:04:37.626 --> 00:04:42.250
one of the fiercest
predators in the region.

00:04:42.792 --> 00:04:48.709
Dr Marisa Tellez, Executive
 Director of the Crocodile
 Research Coalition,

00:04:48.709 --> 00:04:51.250
has been studying these
 animals for years.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:54.083
And she knows exactly
what James is up against.

00:04:54.083 --> 00:04:57.501
MARISA: The American
Alligator have evolved

00:04:57.501 --> 00:05:01.375
a bite force to clamp
down and to hold on.

00:05:01.375 --> 00:05:05.209
A ten foot alligator,
you're looking at a
bite force of about

00:05:05.209 --> 00:05:06.876
1800 pounds per square inch.

00:05:06.876 --> 00:05:11.501
That would be like a
semi truck falling on
your big toe.

00:05:13.709 --> 00:05:17.959
JAMES: All I remember
saying 'you gotta be
(bleep) kidding me.'

00:05:17.959 --> 00:05:19.876
And of course now he's
crunching down

00:05:19.876 --> 00:05:24.918
and I am telling you the
pain, if you go one to
ten it had to be a 15.

00:05:24.918 --> 00:05:27.125
Enough to make your eyes
roll back in your head.

00:05:27.125 --> 00:05:30.209
And I'm screaming, like
I was being murdered.

00:05:30.209 --> 00:05:32.042
(screams)

00:05:32.042 --> 00:05:34.542
TERISA: I've never heard
him scream that way.

00:05:34.542 --> 00:05:35.751
I know something is wrong.

00:05:35.751 --> 00:05:39.709
This is not I've hit my
thumb with a hammer
scream. This is bad.

00:05:39.709 --> 00:05:43.459
And I just freeze.
I'm processing.

00:05:43.459 --> 00:05:45.250
What just happened?
What's wrong?

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:49.042
JAMES: It happened so quick
and he was not letting go.

00:05:49.042 --> 00:05:50.667
I'm in a fight now.

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:55.792
TERISA: What's the matter?
JAMES: She didn't
know I'm in a fight.

00:05:55.792 --> 00:05:57.292
I didn't have time
to tell her that.

00:05:57.292 --> 00:05:59.751
I'm getting bit by a
gator. Hold on honey,
be right there.

00:05:59.751 --> 00:06:03.542
I didn't, no. I yelled
at Tes to stay away.

00:06:03.542 --> 00:06:06.667
Stay back. Stay back.

00:06:06.667 --> 00:06:10.459
The reason why she was
not an experienced hunter.

00:06:10.459 --> 00:06:12.918
And she's not that
good of a shot.

00:06:12.918 --> 00:06:16.626
But the number one
reason I'm afraid
there's a nest there.

00:06:16.626 --> 00:06:19.542
And I did not want
her getting bit.

00:06:19.542 --> 00:06:24.375
NARRATOR: But James' fear of
 an alligator nest is unfounded
 this late in the fall.

00:06:24.375 --> 00:06:27.959
MARISA: During this time
of the year it's past
alligator nesting season.

00:06:27.959 --> 00:06:31.918
It's not that momma
alligator is protecting
her babies.

00:06:31.918 --> 00:06:37.167
She's now pretty much
left the nest and those baby
alligators are by themselves.

00:06:37.167 --> 00:06:45.042
It's very possible that the
Palmetto bush was able to
hide possibly the prey

00:06:45.042 --> 00:06:48.292
that this alligator may
have been protecting.

00:06:48.292 --> 00:06:52.250
JAMES: When he didn't want
let go, I knew he wanted
that leg.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:55.542
And he's crushing it and I
hear popping and popping.

00:06:55.542 --> 00:06:57.417
He severed all the
nerves in my leg.

00:06:57.417 --> 00:06:59.751
That's what the
electricity shock was.

00:06:59.751 --> 00:07:01.834
It was from all the
nerves he severed.

00:07:01.834 --> 00:07:05.459
So, he hit me hard enough
to rip the nerves the
first bite.

00:07:05.459 --> 00:07:08.292
And now he's getting
into the bone.

00:07:08.292 --> 00:07:12.876
He just slowly crush.
It was like a vice.

00:07:12.876 --> 00:07:17.417
Then I'm on his tongue.
And he's like ah.

00:07:17.417 --> 00:07:19.584
And he let out
the growl is there

00:07:19.584 --> 00:07:22.000
his eyes are there
looking right up at me.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.709
And then he's like just
scooting back with me like.

00:07:24.709 --> 00:07:29.000
Oh, no, no, no, no.
And I have this gun
on my shoulder.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.542
But now I'm fighting with
him and grabbing the gun.

00:07:32.542 --> 00:07:33.667
And I started hitting him.

00:07:33.667 --> 00:07:36.834
Hitting him to enough
where that gun started
breaking apart.

00:07:36.834 --> 00:07:39.709
I hit him in the nose. I
hit him on top of the head.

00:07:39.709 --> 00:07:41.375
I hit him in the
eye a few times.

00:07:41.375 --> 00:07:42.501
At the time I didn't think
it was doing anything

00:07:42.501 --> 00:07:46.000
because he was just going
back and forth, it didn't
make a difference.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.125
And course I've got
blood now running
down in his mouth.

00:07:50.125 --> 00:07:57.334
When your whole leg is inside
an animal, you are becoming
part of the food chain.

00:07:57.334 --> 00:08:02.959
Here I am eating the
food that God's created
and taken off this earth.

00:08:02.959 --> 00:08:04.542
Now I'm becoming his prey.

00:08:04.542 --> 00:08:06.792
He was looking at
me as a food source.

00:08:06.792 --> 00:08:08.375
To be eaten alive.

00:08:14.501 --> 00:08:16.959
OLIVIA: I was aware
that they were in
the United States.

00:08:16.959 --> 00:08:22.709
I know they've been
moving up through
Brazil over the years.

00:08:22.709 --> 00:08:25.459
Part of some crazy
experiment.

00:08:27.918 --> 00:08:30.083
Nature can be treacherous.

00:08:30.083 --> 00:08:34.751
I never in my life
thought that I would
live to tell about it.

00:08:39.626 --> 00:08:44.292
NARRATOR: Orlando, Florida.
 It's mild winters and sub
 tropical climate

00:08:44.292 --> 00:08:47.292
make it not just a popular
 vacation destination.

00:08:47.292 --> 00:08:50.042
But also a great spot for
professionals to gather

00:08:50.042 --> 00:08:52.209
for conferences and
 corporate retreats.

00:08:52.209 --> 00:08:57.167
Which is why Dr Olivia
 Affuso and Dr Daheia
 Barr-Anderson have come here

00:08:57.167 --> 00:09:01.792
to attend an annual sports
 medicine conference.

00:09:01.792 --> 00:09:03.125
OLIVIA: I'm Olivia Affuso.

00:09:03.125 --> 00:09:06.876
And I'm an Associate
Professor of Epidemiology

00:09:06.876 --> 00:09:09.584
at the University of
Alabama at Birmingham.

00:09:09.584 --> 00:09:12.417
I'm very passionate about
spending time outside.

00:09:12.417 --> 00:09:15.667
And that has actually
contributed to my work

00:09:15.667 --> 00:09:17.918
in physical activity
with women.

00:09:17.918 --> 00:09:20.209
NARRATOR: It was through her
 work with women in fitness

00:09:20.209 --> 00:09:23.000
that Olivia built her
 friendship with Daheia.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:28.167
OLIVIA: I've known
Daheia for, I don't know,
maybe 15 years or so.

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:32.042
DAHEIA: And I can't
remember when we first
started talking

00:09:32.042 --> 00:09:33.751
and we started
becoming friends.

00:09:33.751 --> 00:09:36.876
But when we did we found out
that we had a lot in common.

00:09:36.876 --> 00:09:41.792
OLIVIA: We bond over you
know engaging in physical
activity with women.

00:09:41.792 --> 00:09:45.959
I'm more of a runner and she
really is an expert in yoga.

00:09:45.959 --> 00:09:49.626
DAHEIA: Then we started
being roommates at our
ACSM annual meetings.

00:09:49.626 --> 00:09:55.209
And so we would always see
each other and reconnect
at least once a year.

00:09:55.209 --> 00:09:58.876
NARRATOR: In May 2019, Olivia
 and Daheia meet up again

00:09:58.876 --> 00:10:02.709
for the American College
of Sports Medicines
annual conference,

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:06.542
along with 6,000 other sports
 medicine practitioners.

00:10:06.542 --> 00:10:11.417
OLIVIA: It's myself
and Daheia and her
mentoree, Scott.

00:10:11.417 --> 00:10:15.834
DAHEIA: It's the end
of May in Orlando and so
the weather is beautiful.

00:10:15.834 --> 00:10:21.876
OLIVIA: Tropical kind of
climate environment with
lots of trees

00:10:21.876 --> 00:10:24.334
and plants and flowers.

00:10:24.334 --> 00:10:27.959
NARRATOR: After a long
 day of meetings, they head
 out for a casual dinner.

00:10:27.959 --> 00:10:35.042
OLIVIA: So, as we were
walking to dinner, I see two
guys that are on a boom lift.

00:10:35.042 --> 00:10:38.709
It appeared that the
two guys were
changing the signs,

00:10:38.709 --> 00:10:43.083
like those banner signs
that you would see in front
of a convention center

00:10:43.083 --> 00:10:45.834
about whatever
event is going on.

00:10:45.834 --> 00:10:49.876
At first nothing seems
out of the ordinary.

00:10:49.876 --> 00:10:53.125
Until I notice them
sort of flailing.

00:10:53.125 --> 00:10:55.292
And then the screaming
starts.

00:10:55.292 --> 00:10:57.250
(screams)

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:00.042
DAHEIA: We all hear the
workers screaming and we
look over

00:11:00.042 --> 00:11:04.417
and we see that they are
kind of surrounded by
this swarm of bees.

00:11:04.417 --> 00:11:10.083
OLIVIA: The swarm of
bees just came out like a
mushroom cloud around them.

00:11:10.083 --> 00:11:11.584
I've never seen
that many bees.

00:11:11.584 --> 00:11:16.375
And they were immediately
attacking the two guys
that were on the lift.

00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:18.959
And of course, they
were screaming.

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:21.876
(screams)

00:11:25.792 --> 00:11:29.792
NARRATOR: As Olivia and
her friends look on, the
workers furiously try

00:11:29.792 --> 00:11:32.876
to protect themselves
 from the swarm of bees.

00:11:32.876 --> 00:11:36.751
But their efforts
seem futile.

00:11:36.751 --> 00:11:39.918
DAHEIA: You could hear the
terror in their voice.

00:11:39.918 --> 00:11:42.375
OLIVIA: It was horrific.
The screaming.

00:11:42.375 --> 00:11:43.876
DAHEIA: There had to
be thousands of bees.

00:11:43.876 --> 00:11:48.709
It was like an opaque
mass, like of a cloud.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:51.042
NARRATOR: The Honeybee.

00:11:51.042 --> 00:11:55.834
A small winged insect
 known for its unique
 social organization

00:11:55.834 --> 00:11:56.709
and honey production.

00:11:56.709 --> 00:12:02.751
The most common bee, the
Western Honeybee, can
live in hives of 80,000.

00:12:02.751 --> 00:12:04.626
They play a crucial
 role in pollination

00:12:04.626 --> 00:12:08.417
and the female bees can
 deliver a painful
 venomous sting

00:12:08.417 --> 00:12:11.667
from a tiny barb
 on their lower abdomen.

00:12:11.667 --> 00:12:18.000
Dr Juliana Rangel, associate
 professor of apiculture at
 Texas A&amp;M University

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.876
has been studying
 honeybees since 2001.

00:12:20.876 --> 00:12:24.751
JULIANA: Honeybees
are insects in the
order Hymenoptera.

00:12:24.751 --> 00:12:28.125
They are cousins
to ants and wasps.

00:12:28.125 --> 00:12:33.250
Probably what Olivia
is observing is a
big honeybee swarm.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:35.959
A honeybee colony is
considered a super organism.

00:12:35.959 --> 00:12:41.167
A higher level entity that
lives together as one unit.

00:12:41.167 --> 00:12:46.292
So, when a worker is going
to sting as a defense
mechanism,

00:12:46.292 --> 00:12:50.083
that is the ultimate way
in which they defend
they colony.

00:12:50.083 --> 00:12:52.292
And of course they
die upon stinging.

00:12:52.292 --> 00:12:54.709
So when they sting, that's
it, they lose their life

00:12:54.709 --> 00:12:59.292
and they're willing to do so
for the good of their colony.

00:12:59.959 --> 00:13:01.250
OLIVIA: They were
very aggressive.

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:05.000
They appeared to have
been resting along
the side of the pole.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:08.125
And when the guys were
trying to change the sign,

00:13:08.125 --> 00:13:12.751
they probably just moved
the sign that was there.

00:13:12.751 --> 00:13:15.501
And I think they
disturbed the bees.

00:13:15.501 --> 00:13:17.667
They just descended
from that pole

00:13:17.667 --> 00:13:23.042
and completely covered
the guys within a
matter of seconds.

00:13:23.042 --> 00:13:26.417
And I knew that they
were in trouble.

00:13:26.959 --> 00:13:29.709
JULIANA: When the lift
workers are peeling
off the banner,

00:13:29.709 --> 00:13:31.542
they are making the
bees aggravated

00:13:31.542 --> 00:13:36.375
because they feel that
they need to defend their
resting site.

00:13:36.375 --> 00:13:42.459
And that's when the whole
swarming and defensive
behavior starts.

00:13:42.542 --> 00:13:45.918
When a colony is ready to
reproduce, it issues a swarm.

00:13:45.918 --> 00:13:52.709
Which is a smaller unit
of bees with a queen that
stops in a temporary place.

00:13:52.709 --> 00:13:54.501
They're defending
that resting place.

00:13:54.501 --> 00:13:58.250
They feel threatened,
they think that they're
probably gonna get eaten

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:01.834
or taken somewhere. And so
they are definitely

00:14:01.834 --> 00:14:04.626
enacting their defensive
behavior to take care

00:14:04.626 --> 00:14:07.292
of the queen and the
rest of the colony.

00:14:07.292 --> 00:14:11.459
(screams)
(simultaneous dialogue)

00:14:12.834 --> 00:14:16.375
DAHEIA: Anyone who knows me
knows that I went bonkers.

00:14:16.375 --> 00:14:18.959
Cause we had two very
different reactions.

00:14:18.959 --> 00:14:22.501
And I started backing away
and going over to the left,

00:14:22.501 --> 00:14:26.334
as far away from
the bees as I can.

00:14:26.334 --> 00:14:28.834
Olivia, she runs
towards the two guys.

00:14:28.834 --> 00:14:34.042
OLIVIA: I knew that I had
to do something because if
they did not get help quick,

00:14:34.042 --> 00:14:35.417
they were going to die.

00:14:35.417 --> 00:14:40.000
I know there's a limit to
how many stings you can
sustain before you die.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.959
And so, I had to move
as quickly as possible to
try to get them some help.

00:14:44.959 --> 00:14:49.876
To try to get them out
of the swarm of bees.

00:14:49.876 --> 00:14:53.042
JULIANA: These bees
definitely seem to be
more aggressive

00:14:53.042 --> 00:14:54.876
than the normal,
gentle honeybee.

00:14:54.876 --> 00:15:00.042
Which leads me to suspect
that they come from a
specific lineage of bees

00:15:00.042 --> 00:15:04.667
that is more aggressive
in general than others.

00:15:06.751 --> 00:15:09.042
OLIVIA: I was like I
have to do something.

00:15:09.042 --> 00:15:13.542
And so, I noticed the
police car on the
side of the street

00:15:13.542 --> 00:15:15.626
and I just immediately
ran for help.

00:15:15.626 --> 00:15:20.834
I just tapped on the window
and I'm like you have to
call an ambulance right now.

00:15:20.834 --> 00:15:23.751
We gotta get those guys down.
We gotta get them down.

00:15:23.751 --> 00:15:28.459
And then I turn around and
I run back over to the left.

00:15:28.459 --> 00:15:33.751
The guy is trying
to use the controls to
come down from the lift.

00:15:33.751 --> 00:15:40.959
As the lift is coming
down, I can see that they are
completely covered with bees.

00:15:40.959 --> 00:15:44.042
Even though they have their
shirts over their heads,

00:15:44.042 --> 00:15:48.167
the bees are still attacking
them through their shirts.

00:15:48.167 --> 00:15:51.417
And on their hands
and just everywhere.

00:15:51.417 --> 00:15:54.209
And they are screaming
bloody murder.

00:15:54.209 --> 00:15:56.292
Help! Help!

00:15:56.292 --> 00:15:59.375
NARRATOR: According to venom
 scientist, Christie Wilcox,

00:15:59.375 --> 00:16:02.042
the pain caused by
 venom is by design.

00:16:02.042 --> 00:16:05.459
CHRISTINE: Bee venoms primary
purpose is to cause pain.

00:16:05.459 --> 00:16:09.042
So, once you've been
stung by a bee, there is
a gland full of venom

00:16:09.042 --> 00:16:15.626
and that whole system
actually rips out of the bee
and stays inside of you.

00:16:15.626 --> 00:16:18.751
And that muscle that pumps
the venom will keep pumping,

00:16:18.751 --> 00:16:22.584
sticking more and more
venom through that
needle into your body.

00:16:22.584 --> 00:16:26.125
So, one bee sting tends
to be fairly harmless.

00:16:26.125 --> 00:16:28.626
When you have hundreds
upon hundreds of bee
stings,

00:16:28.626 --> 00:16:32.334
all of a sudden you are
talking about a very
different dose of venom.

00:16:32.334 --> 00:16:35.459
The danger when you
have hundreds and
hundreds of stings,

00:16:35.459 --> 00:16:37.918
is that you have
this massive release
of histamine.

00:16:37.918 --> 00:16:42.542
And that can cause your
throat to close, your
heart to stop.

00:16:42.542 --> 00:16:45.375
All sorts of very life
threatening things.

00:16:45.375 --> 00:16:48.167
OLIVIA: I could see
that there was a fire
extinguisher.

00:16:48.167 --> 00:16:52.501
And I said to myself I
have to be able to do
something with that.

00:16:52.501 --> 00:16:58.501
As I try to get the fire
extinguisher I'm immediately
covered by bees.

00:16:58.501 --> 00:17:04.542
They were stinging
my head, my arms, my
hands, my legs, my feet.

00:17:04.542 --> 00:17:08.417
Probably upwards of
100 stings.

00:17:08.417 --> 00:17:12.626
I was just on fire.

00:17:16.501 --> 00:17:20.667
JAMES: And when I looked
at his mouth open,
my foot was down in it,

00:17:20.667 --> 00:17:24.417
and his mouth was past
my knee. I knew there
was a problem.

00:17:24.417 --> 00:17:28.209
A major one because he
started pulling me back
and trying to do a roll.

00:17:28.209 --> 00:17:32.709
MARISA: The death roll
is very famous amongst
crocodillians

00:17:32.709 --> 00:17:35.542
in regards of
dismembering prey.

00:17:35.542 --> 00:17:40.584
So, an alligator would
latch on to a piece of prey.

00:17:40.584 --> 00:17:44.918
And the force of the
death roll, it actually
just comes from the snout.

00:17:44.918 --> 00:17:48.417
And the force generated from
the current of the snout,

00:17:48.417 --> 00:17:51.834
it allows the alligator
to dismember prey

00:17:51.834 --> 00:17:55.334
that is much larger than
the alligator itself.

00:17:55.334 --> 00:18:00.542
But it's not just about
dismembering or in a sense
taking chunks out of prey.

00:18:00.542 --> 00:18:05.876
But they'll also use it as
a way to injure an opponent

00:18:05.876 --> 00:18:10.167
so that they could get away
or possibly win a fight.

00:18:10.167 --> 00:18:11.709
JAMES: (screams).

00:18:11.709 --> 00:18:12.959
TERISA: He's
screaming very loud.

00:18:12.959 --> 00:18:15.667
He's making a blood
curdling scream.

00:18:15.667 --> 00:18:18.876
JAMES: Stay back.
TERISA: Get back.

00:18:18.876 --> 00:18:20.834
I'm telling you get back.

00:18:20.834 --> 00:18:22.834
What happened?
What's wrong?

00:18:22.834 --> 00:18:26.542
JAMES: I rolled with him
twice. It felt like hours.

00:18:26.542 --> 00:18:28.792
But it wasn't, it was
only a few minutes.

00:18:28.792 --> 00:18:32.042
And then hit him
again, he left go.

00:18:32.042 --> 00:18:34.250
And that was my break.
It was like oh.

00:18:35.292 --> 00:18:38.792
I knew it was bad cause
I could just feel the
warmth of the blood

00:18:38.792 --> 00:18:40.083
running down my
leg into my boot.

00:18:40.083 --> 00:18:42.667
And the right side of
my leg I couldn't feel.

00:18:42.667 --> 00:18:46.751
Well I knew if I did
not tourniquet my
leg I'm a dead man.

00:18:46.751 --> 00:18:51.334
So, I turned around
and ripped my belt
off, dropped my gun

00:18:51.334 --> 00:18:52.709
and started tightening
up my leg.

00:18:52.709 --> 00:18:56.667
And he came running up
from behind me and grabbed
me on the back of the leg.

00:18:56.667 --> 00:19:00.792
A second time. Now I
can't see him. That
was my mistake.

00:19:00.792 --> 00:19:01.667
Should never have
turned my back.

00:19:01.667 --> 00:19:06.417
MARISA: Turning your
back to a predator is
a submissive position.

00:19:06.417 --> 00:19:11.042
And so, that alligator
likely came back for
him just to say

00:19:11.042 --> 00:19:12.792
I have weakened my opponent.

00:19:12.792 --> 00:19:17.042
My opponent is submissive
but I'm gonna go one more
time to let him know

00:19:17.042 --> 00:19:20.542
that I am the winner in
this particular battle.

00:19:20.542 --> 00:19:22.292
JAMES: Then when he did
that I said that's it.

00:19:22.292 --> 00:19:26.209
I'm just gonna shoot him
and I was gonna either
blow my leg off

00:19:26.209 --> 00:19:29.501
or blow him away with my
leg in it. I didn't care.

00:19:39.042 --> 00:19:42.501
NARRATOR: In an effort
 to kill the alligator and
 minimize damage to this leg,

00:19:42.501 --> 00:19:49.125
James shoves the gun barrel
 as far into the alligator's
 mouth as he can.

00:19:49.125 --> 00:19:51.125
JAMES: I stuck the
barrel inside his mouth

00:19:51.125 --> 00:19:56.542
and all of a sudden he
let go and he took off.

00:19:56.542 --> 00:19:58.626
MARISA: James using
his rifle

00:19:58.626 --> 00:20:01.751
and pounding against
the back of the throat,

00:20:01.751 --> 00:20:04.876
most likely made the
alligator release.

00:20:04.876 --> 00:20:10.375
In the back of a crocodilians
throat, is a sensitive area
called the palatal valve.

00:20:10.375 --> 00:20:15.167
It prevents water from
going in while swimming
around or hunting.

00:20:15.167 --> 00:20:20.209
The alligators would
definitely not want that
to be damaged because

00:20:20.209 --> 00:20:24.209
that would impact its
ability to be able to
successfully hunt,

00:20:24.209 --> 00:20:27.751
especially under water when
it's trying to catch fish

00:20:27.751 --> 00:20:29.584
trying to catch turtles.

00:20:31.209 --> 00:20:34.083
TERISA: He's backing up
to get out of that area

00:20:34.083 --> 00:20:38.209
and then he's telling me
give me your belt. I
need your belt.

00:20:38.209 --> 00:20:43.542
And I'm dumping the gear.
I'm throwing my rifle down.

00:20:43.542 --> 00:20:44.876
I'm getting this belt.

00:20:44.876 --> 00:20:48.542
And then he's tying the
belt around his leg.

00:20:48.542 --> 00:20:52.334
And he's like call 911.
JAMES: Call 911.

00:20:52.334 --> 00:20:54.709
And she knew it was bad cause
she heard me screaming.

00:20:54.709 --> 00:20:57.417
And she just lost it.
I had to grab her,

00:20:57.417 --> 00:21:01.042
snap her out of it and
say you have to gotta
pay attention

00:21:01.042 --> 00:21:02.375
or I'm gonna lose
my life here.

00:21:02.375 --> 00:21:06.417
Get on that phone. Please.
TERISA: I can't.

00:21:06.417 --> 00:21:07.042
(breathes heavily).

00:21:07.042 --> 00:21:10.083
I made a couple of
attempts to get 911.
I couldn't.

00:21:10.083 --> 00:21:13.667
The cell service
there was very spotty.

00:21:13.667 --> 00:21:15.959
JAMES: And she goes there's
no signal. I said I know.

00:21:15.959 --> 00:21:19.375
Just keep calling.
Keep calling.

00:21:19.375 --> 00:21:25.375
I was in a cypress area
covered with trees, a
canopy of trees.

00:21:25.375 --> 00:21:26.834
I'm not getting
out like that.

00:21:26.834 --> 00:21:28.042
But there is no
open area either.

00:21:28.042 --> 00:21:34.083
And I knew I had to get to
where we could be spotted.
That was my number one.

00:21:35.375 --> 00:21:39.834
TERISA: So we start
walking and b-line it
through the water.

00:21:39.834 --> 00:21:42.042
JAMES: When we're walking
she's calling and calling.

00:21:42.042 --> 00:21:42.876
We get the signal,
lose signal.

00:21:42.876 --> 00:21:47.083
And it's spotty cause we're
on the Martin County line
and Palm Beach County line.

00:21:47.083 --> 00:21:50.209
TERISA: I was scared
cause now the water
is up to my knees

00:21:50.209 --> 00:21:54.167
and it was pouring into
my boots and I know
what's in this water.

00:21:54.167 --> 00:21:59.292
There is the alligators
and there's rattle snakes
and water moccasins.

00:21:59.292 --> 00:22:01.751
Things that'll get you.
And he's bleeding.

00:22:01.751 --> 00:22:05.209
So, when you're bleeding and
there are hungry animals,

00:22:05.209 --> 00:22:10.626
the chances are higher
that something else is
now gonna happen.

00:22:15.417 --> 00:22:19.709
NARRATOR: Back in Orlando,
 Dr Olivia Affuso tries to
 save two workers

00:22:19.709 --> 00:22:22.792
being attacked by an
aggressive swarm of bees.

00:22:22.792 --> 00:22:26.918
She grabs a fire
 extinguisher to spray
 the bees off the men.

00:22:26.918 --> 00:22:30.584
But now she's under attack.
 And the pain is intense.

00:22:30.584 --> 00:22:32.375
OLIVIA: So, I've
been stung before.

00:22:32.375 --> 00:22:37.292
So, it's kind of like
you know going to get
a needle at the doctor.

00:22:37.292 --> 00:22:40.751
But now imagine hundreds
of needles at once.

00:22:40.751 --> 00:22:48.125
And I just focused my mind
and stay on the task at hand.

00:22:48.125 --> 00:22:52.459
I couldn't leave them.
And I got it out.

00:22:52.459 --> 00:22:53.959
And I took the fire
extinguisher,

00:22:53.959 --> 00:22:56.459
I pulled the pin and I
turned it on the guys,

00:22:56.459 --> 00:23:01.042
trying to spray them so
that they could escape
from the bees

00:23:01.042 --> 00:23:04.584
to be able to get out.

00:23:04.584 --> 00:23:06.709
JULIANA: When Olivia grabbed
that fire extinguisher

00:23:06.709 --> 00:23:10.626
what she didn't know is that
she was doing something
similar to the effects

00:23:10.626 --> 00:23:12.667
of the smoke created that
we use in bee keeping.

00:23:12.667 --> 00:23:17.542
What the smoke is doing
is dissipating the release
of the alarm pheromone.

00:23:17.542 --> 00:23:22.959
So, it's calming the bees
down and it's confusing them
because it is mixing

00:23:22.959 --> 00:23:27.709
the smell of the smoke
with the smell of the
alarm pheromone.

00:23:27.709 --> 00:23:31.792
Only she didn't know
that this was only a
temporary fix.

00:23:32.626 --> 00:23:37.542
OLIVIA: And then I ran away
from the bucket, turned
around, closed my eyes.

00:23:37.542 --> 00:23:40.125
DAHEIA: She turns the fire
extinguisher on herself

00:23:40.125 --> 00:23:44.876
and sprays herself in
the face and all over.

00:23:44.876 --> 00:23:48.417
And it was just a blur.

00:23:48.417 --> 00:23:50.959
It's a blur of like all
of this that's going on.

00:23:50.959 --> 00:23:55.626
OLIVIA: And then I dropped
fire extinguisher right
there on the sidewalk

00:23:55.626 --> 00:23:59.042
and I run to the
convention center.

00:23:59.042 --> 00:24:03.292
And that's when my friend,
Daheia came to save me.

00:24:03.292 --> 00:24:08.000
And she starts swatting
my head to get the bees
off of me.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:14.375
DAHEIA: I just start trying
to fan them away and I know
I'm smacking her in the head.

00:24:14.375 --> 00:24:20.209
Bees are getting angrier and
angrier because we're hitting
them and swatting them.

00:24:20.209 --> 00:24:22.042
JULIANA: That actually
aggravates the bees more.

00:24:22.042 --> 00:24:27.459
Fast movements and high
contrasts of movements
actually gets them very

00:24:27.459 --> 00:24:32.501
aggravated and they want
to sting at a faster rate.

00:24:32.501 --> 00:24:33.417
DAHEIA: They're still
swarming around.

00:24:33.417 --> 00:24:41.250
My thought is we have to get
these bees off of Olivia and
we need to get out of here.

00:24:45.375 --> 00:24:49.834
NARRATOR: After surviving
 an alligator attack, James
 Boyce and his wife, Terisa,

00:24:49.834 --> 00:24:55.042
struggle through the dense
 growth of the JW Corbett
 Wildlife Preserve.

00:24:55.042 --> 00:25:01.918
James, losing blood
 rapidly, is too weak
 to go any further.

00:25:01.918 --> 00:25:05.751
TERISA: Finally I
get through to 911.

00:25:05.751 --> 00:25:06.876
I'm JW Corbett.

00:25:06.876 --> 00:25:11.751
My husband has got bit
really bad on the leg
by an alligator.

00:25:11.751 --> 00:25:16.584
And because we're on
the border line of these
two management areas,

00:25:16.584 --> 00:25:17.959
they're two different
counties.

00:25:17.959 --> 00:25:23.667
And I was afraid because it
took so long to get through.
Please don't transfer me.

00:25:23.667 --> 00:25:25.209
911 DISPATCHER: Hang
one one second ma'am.

00:25:25.209 --> 00:25:27.626
Are you in Dupuis or Corbett?

00:25:27.626 --> 00:25:29.083
Cause those are two
different counties.

00:25:29.083 --> 00:25:30.918
TERISA: I'm in Corbett.
911 DISPATCHER: Okay.

00:25:30.918 --> 00:25:34.250
Alright, stay on the
line, I gotta transfer
you to Palm Beach County.

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:35.083
Stay on the line,
don't hang up.

00:25:35.083 --> 00:25:36.542
TERISA: You cannot,
please don't transfer me.

00:25:36.542 --> 00:25:42.751
I'm like can I just get
anybody, somebody to
come and help us.

00:25:42.751 --> 00:25:46.042
NARRATOR: After a few
minutes Terisa is finally
able to relay

00:25:46.042 --> 00:25:47.375
their approximate location.

00:25:47.375 --> 00:25:51.501
Operators immediately
 dispatch a helicopter
 rescue.

00:25:51.501 --> 00:25:57.209
TERISA: The 911 operator
wanted me to look and
verify the amount of blood.

00:25:57.209 --> 00:26:01.459
And he asked me to lift his
leg up, elevate his leg.

00:26:01.459 --> 00:26:04.417
I pick up his leg to put
it up over my knees

00:26:04.417 --> 00:26:08.501
and the blood just starts
pouring out of his boot.

00:26:08.501 --> 00:26:10.000
And I'm like this is bad.

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:15.167
JAMES: I looked down at
my leg and I could see
bone and fat.

00:26:15.167 --> 00:26:19.542
And veins and blood
just squirting out.

00:26:19.542 --> 00:26:23.959
TERISA: And then he looks
at me and he goes this is
it, you know that right?

00:26:23.959 --> 00:26:25.751
So, he's saying
goodbye to me.

00:26:25.751 --> 00:26:28.584
And he's talking to me. And

00:26:28.584 --> 00:26:30.542
I'm not listening.
I refused.

00:26:30.542 --> 00:26:33.375
I wasn't gonna let him
die. I just wasn't.

00:26:33.375 --> 00:26:37.459
JAMES: I know my wife. And
I'd see at the back of her
mind, through her eyes.

00:26:37.459 --> 00:26:39.375
She kept telling me
I wasn't gonna die.

00:26:39.375 --> 00:26:41.542
But she thought I was.

00:26:41.542 --> 00:26:43.709
And I knew too.
I thought so too.

00:26:44.584 --> 00:26:46.918
Because I do have
a blood condition.

00:26:46.918 --> 00:26:49.709
And it's hemochromatosis.
It's very rare.

00:26:49.709 --> 00:26:52.542
The blood count on your
platelets is major.

00:26:52.542 --> 00:26:58.876
And mine normally is
at 110. Persons normal
is 275, 350.

00:26:58.876 --> 00:27:04.334
So, if I lose a lot of
blood, my body can't
make it up.

00:27:04.334 --> 00:27:06.918
And she's on the phone.
People couldn't find us.

00:27:06.918 --> 00:27:08.918
We'd lose him and I
said to hell with it.

00:27:08.918 --> 00:27:11.542
I said get on the phone,
ring and call all the boys.

00:27:11.542 --> 00:27:14.751
I just wanted to make
sure everybody knew
how much I loved them.

00:27:14.751 --> 00:27:17.834
Cause it gets real. And
if you got anything to
say, say it now.

00:27:17.834 --> 00:27:21.834
I called, I think it was
Christopher, and I said look
man, I got bit by a gator.

00:27:21.834 --> 00:27:25.792
And he thought I was joking.
I said shut up and listen.

00:27:25.792 --> 00:27:26.709
I don't know what's
gonna happen.

00:27:26.709 --> 00:27:31.751
I said I love you, I said my
goodbyes to him. I gotta go.
Call the next one.

00:27:31.751 --> 00:27:37.334
TERISA: The kids is hard
for me. He says I love you.

00:27:37.334 --> 00:27:38.959
I'm proud of you.

00:27:38.959 --> 00:27:44.542
You're gonna grow up to be
a great person. I want you
to live your life.

00:27:44.542 --> 00:27:47.542
I want you to do the
things you wanna do.

00:27:47.542 --> 00:27:50.000
And then he's done.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.792
JAMES: And once I got that
out, the rest is easy.

00:27:52.792 --> 00:27:56.542
And I mean you're already
going out because you're
passing out.

00:27:56.542 --> 00:27:59.751
You're losing all your blood.
There's no help in sight.

00:27:59.751 --> 00:28:04.417
So, you got that working
for you and you just
say well I'm just tired.

00:28:04.417 --> 00:28:07.209
You accept it and I did.
100 percent.

00:28:07.209 --> 00:28:12.709
Said I'm gonna accept
the death. I was
ready to go then.

00:28:15.709 --> 00:28:18.626
NARRATOR: Am ambulance
 takes the lift workers
 to the hospital.

00:28:18.626 --> 00:28:22.918
But Olivia is left with
 over 100 stings from
 her rescue effort.

00:28:22.918 --> 00:28:27.083
Her friends, Daheia and
 Scott, are able to get
 her into the safety

00:28:27.083 --> 00:28:28.083
of the convention center.

00:28:28.083 --> 00:28:33.209
OLIVIA: I sat down in a
chair to wait on
medical assistance.

00:28:33.209 --> 00:28:35.292
I start to look
down at my arm

00:28:35.292 --> 00:28:41.918
and I just can see all
of these little stingers
on my arm.

00:28:41.918 --> 00:28:48.542
And we start picking them
out, at least immediate ones
that they see in my face

00:28:48.542 --> 00:28:49.709
and on my arm.

00:28:49.709 --> 00:28:53.709
Because you know the
bees had stung me here
in my face.

00:28:53.709 --> 00:28:56.000
And there were
stingers still there.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.542
JULIANA: When Daheia goes
and plucks the stingers out,

00:28:58.542 --> 00:29:02.501
that in theory is a good
idea depending on how
quickly you're doing it.

00:29:02.501 --> 00:29:07.959
Because it takes about 20 to
30 seconds for the full
contents of the venom gland

00:29:07.959 --> 00:29:10.167
to be injected
into the flesh.

00:29:10.167 --> 00:29:14.542
Depending on how long
Daheia took to get rid
of the stingers,

00:29:14.542 --> 00:29:17.209
it may or may not
have helped.

00:29:17.209 --> 00:29:23.542
OLIVIA: And now that the
adrenalin was starting to come
down from what I had done,

00:29:23.542 --> 00:29:26.918
then the pain started
to come with it.

00:29:26.918 --> 00:29:27.792
I just felt burning.

00:29:27.792 --> 00:29:33.834
After you know the stingers
were removed I just
felt like I was on fire.

00:29:33.834 --> 00:29:35.792
DAHEIA: Her skin isn't
really dark right,

00:29:35.792 --> 00:29:41.709
and so you could see the
redness starting to appear
on her arms and on her neck.

00:29:41.709 --> 00:29:46.626
But she wasn't complaining.
She wasn't yelling. She
wasn't moaning.

00:29:46.626 --> 00:29:52.542
And so I think we all
underestimated how
much in pain she was.

00:29:52.542 --> 00:29:55.918
And how urgent she
needed care.

00:29:55.918 --> 00:30:00.459
OLIVIA: And my blood
pressure started
climbing really high.

00:30:00.459 --> 00:30:05.542
And I've never had high
blood pressure in my life.

00:30:13.042 --> 00:30:16.000
TERISA: This is serious
now. I'm scared.

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:18.083
He's laying down.
He's done.

00:30:18.083 --> 00:30:24.167
And then I hear a helicopter
and I get up and I'm looking.

00:30:24.167 --> 00:30:25.959
And here it comes.

00:30:25.959 --> 00:30:27.792
And it's going
and it's circling.

00:30:27.792 --> 00:30:33.501
And it looks like it's coming
and I'm screaming, here.

00:30:35.083 --> 00:30:38.250
It turns. And it goes
the other way.

00:30:38.250 --> 00:30:43.417
I'm on with 911 and I'm
telling him tell them
to turn around.

00:30:43.417 --> 00:30:46.918
Tell him to turn around,
tell him to come back.
He went the wrong way.

00:30:46.918 --> 00:30:49.709
Tell him to bang a left.

00:30:49.709 --> 00:30:51.501
I'm heartbroken.

00:30:51.501 --> 00:30:53.918
That was our last chance.

00:30:53.918 --> 00:30:56.250
JAMES: It was like looking
for a needle in a haystack.

00:30:56.250 --> 00:30:57.834
I don't care, you
could have orange,

00:30:57.834 --> 00:31:02.083
you can have a lightning rod,
you can have what you want.

00:31:02.083 --> 00:31:04.042
They can't find you.

00:31:04.042 --> 00:31:07.626
TERISA: So my husband
says we should shoot
shots into the ground.

00:31:07.626 --> 00:31:11.584
Somebody could hear us. So,
I put the shells in the gun.

00:31:11.584 --> 00:31:14.876
(gunshots)

00:31:14.876 --> 00:31:16.876
JAMES: She was firing
it like a champ.

00:31:16.876 --> 00:31:19.918
TERISA: And I'm screaming help.

00:31:19.918 --> 00:31:20.542
Help.

00:31:20.542 --> 00:31:24.918
TERISA: (shouts) Somebody.
(Cries) Help me.

00:31:24.918 --> 00:31:27.083
Here comes the
helicopter again.

00:31:27.083 --> 00:31:30.459
I'm like okay, maybe
they'll see us.

00:31:30.459 --> 00:31:34.375
(Shouts) Over here.
Over here.

00:31:34.375 --> 00:31:39.000
As the helicopter's coming
here comes the swamp buggy.

00:31:39.834 --> 00:31:45.584
NARRATOR: Terisa's gun shots
 alert fellow hunters, Danny
 McCullen and his family.

00:31:45.584 --> 00:31:50.375
TERISA: And I'm just
like, thank god.

00:31:50.375 --> 00:31:54.250
Just thank god there's somebody
here, there's somebody here.

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:58.876
And the lady that was with
them came right to me.

00:31:58.876 --> 00:32:01.334
And she just grabbed me.

00:32:01.334 --> 00:32:04.584
And I just, I fell
to the ground.

00:32:04.584 --> 00:32:09.667
So, Danny runs back to his
buggy and grabs some zip ties.

00:32:09.667 --> 00:32:11.876
Our tourniquet just,
it was wet.

00:32:11.876 --> 00:32:14.209
It was stretched,
it wasn't holding.

00:32:14.209 --> 00:32:16.209
They just zip tie his leg.

00:32:16.209 --> 00:32:20.000
JAMES: Woke up to Danny
McCullen and his family

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.667
zip tying my legs with
the EZ Zip Ties.

00:32:22.667 --> 00:32:28.709
They use them out there
for tying up kegs.
It didn't stop it.

00:32:28.709 --> 00:32:33.125
Instead of squirting
out six inches it's just
running down my leg now.

00:32:33.125 --> 00:32:34.042
Cause I was losing
so much blood.

00:32:34.042 --> 00:32:37.876
I started getting shivers.
I was going into shock.

00:32:37.876 --> 00:32:38.876
TERISA: So, we're going
through the woods

00:32:38.876 --> 00:32:43.042
and I'm holding my hand
and trying not to get
hit by the trees.

00:32:43.042 --> 00:32:44.876
And I'm just praying.

00:32:44.876 --> 00:32:49.209
NARRATOR: Finally they
 reach a clearing where
 a helicopter can land.

00:32:49.209 --> 00:32:51.209
JAMES: Course I'm
passing in and out.

00:32:51.209 --> 00:32:54.709
Felt like a long ride
to the helicopter.

00:32:54.709 --> 00:32:58.209
We got in it, it was
raining, it's windy.

00:32:58.209 --> 00:32:59.334
I remember going up and
I said you know what?

00:32:59.334 --> 00:33:03.876
Looked at my wife and said you
know if I don't die here I'm
gonna die in this helicopter.

00:33:03.876 --> 00:33:04.918
Pilot was like this
is bad weather.

00:33:04.918 --> 00:33:10.584
And I'm like yeah, this is
it. And if we're gonna die.
This is meant to be.

00:33:11.959 --> 00:33:15.876
NARRATOR: As Olivia
 suffers the effects of
 a 100 plus bee stings,

00:33:15.876 --> 00:33:19.918
outside the convention
 center, the swarm of
 bees attacks.

00:33:19.918 --> 00:33:23.918
OLIVIA: There were tons of
other people coming from
the conference as well

00:33:23.918 --> 00:33:29.083
and they were stung. And,
many of them ran into the
building with us.

00:33:29.083 --> 00:33:29.751
DAHEIA: It was insane.

00:33:29.751 --> 00:33:34.918
One lady she was walking
through and the bees
attacked her.

00:33:34.918 --> 00:33:37.626
And so, she you know
is trying to fan them.

00:33:37.626 --> 00:33:40.292
And so, she runs
try to get indoors.

00:33:40.292 --> 00:33:41.959
And there's a lot going on.

00:33:41.959 --> 00:33:44.876
(ambulance sirens)

00:33:44.876 --> 00:33:45.792
NARRATOR: EMT's
 quickly arrive.

00:33:45.792 --> 00:33:50.751
But with the chaos going on
 around her, Olivia must wait
 to receive medical attention.

00:33:50.751 --> 00:33:54.542
DAHEIA: You got the security
guard who's saying I'm
having difficulty breathing.

00:33:54.542 --> 00:33:59.209
And then you have the
other lady who says
I'm allergic to bees.

00:33:59.209 --> 00:34:00.918
I could die from a
bee sting.

00:34:00.918 --> 00:34:03.250
And so, she wasn't
triaged first right?

00:34:03.250 --> 00:34:05.334
They were dealing
with the other two.

00:34:05.334 --> 00:34:09.167
OLIVIA: I was the one that
sustained the most stings

00:34:09.167 --> 00:34:11.626
but they were attending
to other people.

00:34:11.626 --> 00:34:19.209
And I understand that
but every time the wave
of fire came to me,

00:34:19.209 --> 00:34:22.209
I became a less
understanding.

00:34:22.209 --> 00:34:23.751
I just had to endure.

00:34:24.042 --> 00:34:25.542
JULIANA: When a bee
stings a person,

00:34:25.542 --> 00:34:30.000
she can either have an
allergic reaction or a
toxic reaction.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:33.792
The allergic reaction is the
one that most people have,

00:34:33.792 --> 00:34:35.542
which is localized swelling,

00:34:35.542 --> 00:34:39.209
a little bit of pain,
redness around the area.

00:34:39.209 --> 00:34:41.501
And potentially can
last for a few days.

00:34:41.501 --> 00:34:48.042
For some people one or a few
stings of a honeybee can
cause a toxic reaction

00:34:48.042 --> 00:34:50.125
that can lead to
anaphylactic shock.

00:34:50.125 --> 00:34:53.751
DAHEIA: And then they
started with Olivia
and they hooked her up.

00:34:53.751 --> 00:34:57.334
We had to take her pulse and
check her blood pressure.

00:34:57.334 --> 00:34:59.125
And her blood pressure
was really high.

00:34:59.125 --> 00:35:03.542
The upper like 150's,
over 90. It was really,
really high.

00:35:03.542 --> 00:35:09.876
It started to dawn on me I
think at that point in time,
she was in a lot of pain.

00:35:09.876 --> 00:35:11.876
NARRATOR: Requiring
 additional medical aid,

00:35:11.876 --> 00:35:13.751
Olivia is rushed to
 the nearest hospital.

00:35:13.751 --> 00:35:19.584
OLIVIA: I am still on fire.
My head is killing me.
I'm in extreme pain.

00:35:19.584 --> 00:35:22.459
I'm hyperventilating. My
blood pressure is going up.

00:35:22.459 --> 00:35:27.751
And I'm just wanting
something for this pain.

00:35:27.751 --> 00:35:31.125
I felt like I was
going to die.

00:35:35.876 --> 00:35:38.042
OLIVIA: I felt something
crawling in my hair.

00:35:38.042 --> 00:35:42.209
And I'm like is there
a bee in my hair?

00:35:42.209 --> 00:35:44.042
So, I reach up there and
I feel something moving.

00:35:44.042 --> 00:35:48.167
So, then I grabbed that part
of my hair and I hold it.

00:35:48.167 --> 00:35:49.626
And I throw it to the floor.

00:35:49.626 --> 00:35:52.542
And sure enough there was a
bee and it was still alive.

00:35:52.542 --> 00:35:56.959
The assistant quickly
used one of those little
cups, specimen cups.

00:35:56.959 --> 00:35:59.876
And he grabs it and twists
it and he runs out with it.

00:35:59.876 --> 00:36:03.250
And he's showing all the
people at the nurses station.

00:36:03.250 --> 00:36:07.209
Eventually the nurse comes
in and she hooks up the IV.

00:36:07.209 --> 00:36:11.209
She has the three different
combinations of medication.

00:36:11.209 --> 00:36:13.834
And she's going to
administer them.

00:36:13.834 --> 00:36:15.626
One is like Benadryl.

00:36:15.626 --> 00:36:19.751
Another is like an
anti-inflammatory.
That's good.

00:36:19.751 --> 00:36:23.334
And then the third one
is a stomach medication

00:36:23.334 --> 00:36:25.667
that they use as a
secondary anti-histamine.

00:36:25.667 --> 00:36:30.083
As soon as she injects
that I started vomiting
everywhere.

00:36:30.083 --> 00:36:32.542
And I couldn't believe that
I had anything to vomit.

00:36:32.542 --> 00:36:35.000
Remember I never
made it to dinner.

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:38.125
NARRATOR: After the
 medication, Olivia
 is discharged

00:36:38.125 --> 00:36:41.000
and walks back to the
 hotel with Daheia.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:45.375
DAHEIA: The next day right,
she's an Epidemiologist,
and we're investigators.

00:36:45.375 --> 00:36:48.375
And so she wanted to
investigate the scene.

00:36:48.375 --> 00:36:49.375
And so she goes back.

00:36:49.375 --> 00:36:53.250
She was walking around
with the bee that was
taken out of her hair.

00:36:53.250 --> 00:36:58.042
OLIVIA: I run into the other
person who had been stung.

00:36:58.042 --> 00:37:04.042
And she said to me one
of the exhibitors is a
beekeeper.

00:37:04.042 --> 00:37:05.876
Who is very familiar
with the bees.

00:37:05.876 --> 00:37:11.626
DAHEIA: And so, she
pulls it out and the guy
identifies it for her.

00:37:11.626 --> 00:37:19.542
OLIVIA: And he said those
were Africanized killer bees.

00:37:19.542 --> 00:37:20.834
JULIANA: Based on
all of these facts,

00:37:20.834 --> 00:37:24.209
the fact that there were
so many stings happening
at the same time,

00:37:24.209 --> 00:37:28.375
the fact that there was a
very high amount of alarm
pheromone being released,

00:37:28.375 --> 00:37:35.626
and a very wide active space,
leads me to believe that
these bees were Africanized.

00:37:35.626 --> 00:37:39.125
NARRATOR: Africanized
 honeybees often called
 killer bees,

00:37:39.125 --> 00:37:42.250
are a hybrid of European
and African descent.

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:44.584
Bred in Brazil to increase
 honey production,

00:37:44.584 --> 00:37:49.209
the Africanized honeybee
is known to be more
aggressively defensive.

00:37:49.209 --> 00:37:52.542
And has caused over
 a 1,000 human deaths.

00:37:52.542 --> 00:37:57.876
JULIANA: If you are unlucky
enough to get one of these
aggressive swarms of bees

00:37:57.876 --> 00:38:04.501
riled up, what you need to
do is run as fast as you
can and seek shelter.

00:38:04.501 --> 00:38:07.375
Unfortunately what
happened to these
workers was unavoidable

00:38:07.375 --> 00:38:13.167
because they were unable to
move from the boom and the
bees will continue to sting.

00:38:13.167 --> 00:38:16.834
There are a few stinging
deaths every year.

00:38:16.834 --> 00:38:20.167
Most likely because a
person was unable to move.

00:38:20.167 --> 00:38:24.792
What Olivia did was
heroic and she helped save
the lives of two people.

00:38:24.792 --> 00:38:30.918
DAHEIA: I probably won't
run towards danger unless
I know I can do something.

00:38:30.918 --> 00:38:33.501
If I'm ever in a
dangerous situation,

00:38:33.501 --> 00:38:36.876
when I am not equipped to
do something about it,

00:38:36.876 --> 00:38:41.000
then I'm with someone
who is equipped.

00:38:41.209 --> 00:38:46.209
OLIVIA: The main thing I
learned from this whole
experience is that

00:38:46.209 --> 00:38:50.667
I am far stronger than
I probably give myself
credit for.

00:38:50.667 --> 00:38:58.459
And probably also it
confirms that I really do
have a high pain tolerance.

00:38:59.250 --> 00:39:02.542
NARRATOR: Back in Palm beach,
 James Boyce fights to survive

00:39:02.542 --> 00:39:04.375
the 45 minute flight
 to the hospital.

00:39:04.375 --> 00:39:09.876
Paramedics give medication
 for the pain but the wounds
 to his leg are extensive.

00:39:09.876 --> 00:39:12.584
JAMES: It was mangled. It
was just a mangled piece
of meat.

00:39:12.584 --> 00:39:14.250
All of this water
getting to it,

00:39:14.250 --> 00:39:18.459
dirty water and infection
and it's just hanging.

00:39:18.459 --> 00:39:22.626
TERISA: From the time he was
bit to the time we arrived
and they got him

00:39:22.626 --> 00:39:26.876
into the trauma room, it
was close to three hours.

00:39:26.876 --> 00:39:30.000
JAMES: And they had the
team of doctors and nurses.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:31.083
They cut all my clothes off.

00:39:31.083 --> 00:39:37.125
The doctor himself, he stuck
his four fingers in that hole.

00:39:37.125 --> 00:39:39.209
To see how deep it went.
And they would disappear.

00:39:39.209 --> 00:39:43.709
And I screamed in pain.
He like give that man a
shot of morphine.

00:39:43.709 --> 00:39:44.876
Let's get him into surgery.

00:39:44.876 --> 00:39:49.584
NARRATOR: Because of
 James' hemochromatosis,
 he's at a much higher risk

00:39:49.584 --> 00:39:51.125
for severe complications.

00:39:51.125 --> 00:39:55.959
A normal platelet count
 ranges from 275 to 350.

00:39:55.959 --> 00:39:58.000
James' is dangerously low.

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:00.042
JAMES: And he looked at the
blood work as down to 50.

00:40:00.042 --> 00:40:04.334
If you're down to 45,
40, you start bleeding
out of every orifice.

00:40:04.334 --> 00:40:07.125
Your nose, your eyes,
your mouth. Then you die.

00:40:07.125 --> 00:40:08.501
And it was getting
to that point.

00:40:08.501 --> 00:40:12.375
TERISA: I get in the door.
I'm greeted with the
Sheriff's department

00:40:12.375 --> 00:40:18.751
and the game wardens.
They have a gator now
that's bitten a human.

00:40:18.751 --> 00:40:25.542
In most instances they
have to remove that
animal from the wild.

00:40:25.542 --> 00:40:30.626
It's now considered
even more dangerous
than it already is.

00:40:30.626 --> 00:40:36.209
And by the time I get
into the room they're
wheeling him out.

00:40:36.209 --> 00:40:44.000
So I never truly knew
how bad it was until they
pulled him out of surgery.

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:46.959
NARRATOR: Despite the
 extensive damage to his leg,

00:40:46.959 --> 00:40:48.375
it could have been
 a lot worse.

00:40:48.375 --> 00:40:54.083
JAMES: The boot did save
my leg. And it was made
for snakes.

00:40:54.083 --> 00:40:54.876
Snake proof.

00:40:54.876 --> 00:40:57.709
When he bit on top of
my leg, he bit high,

00:40:57.709 --> 00:41:01.834
just enough where he covered
the top of that boot too.

00:41:01.834 --> 00:41:03.959
When he let go he ripped
the top of the boot.

00:41:03.959 --> 00:41:06.375
And then he got the
bottom of the boot too.

00:41:06.375 --> 00:41:11.042
But if it wasn't for that
kevlar boot, he would have
had 100 percent control

00:41:11.042 --> 00:41:12.792
of that leg and I
would have lost it.

00:41:12.792 --> 00:41:15.042
And it's definitely
saved me the second bite.

00:41:15.042 --> 00:41:19.709
Cause the second bite
he got the back of my
leg where the calf was.

00:41:19.709 --> 00:41:23.667
MARISA: So, if you're
going out hunting, let's
say out in the swamps.

00:41:23.667 --> 00:41:27.292
One it is good to have
another person with you.

00:41:27.292 --> 00:41:31.000
So, James's wife
was ten feet behind.

00:41:31.000 --> 00:41:32.834
It's possible if she
was closer to James,

00:41:32.834 --> 00:41:36.501
this attack may not
have happened because the
alligator's like oh no,

00:41:36.501 --> 00:41:38.167
two against one, mm-mm,
not gonna happen.

00:41:38.167 --> 00:41:45.501
The other thing that James
could have done is if there
was a long stick, a long pole,

00:41:45.501 --> 00:41:48.584
is just to kind of poke
around in front of him.

00:41:48.584 --> 00:41:52.918
So, if he did scare
something, something's
gonna lash at the pole

00:41:52.918 --> 00:41:56.918
and not necessarily
lash at his leg.

00:41:56.918 --> 00:41:58.959
TERISA: I almost
lost my husband.

00:42:01.459 --> 00:42:05.375
And I almost lost the
father of my kids.

00:42:05.918 --> 00:42:07.709
They need him.
JAMES: People ask well

00:42:07.709 --> 00:42:12.834
you know were you scared
of dying? No, I wasn't.

00:42:12.834 --> 00:42:14.501
It's funny how you
make peace real quick.

00:42:14.501 --> 00:42:18.334
And you start really
thinking about you
know your family.

00:42:18.334 --> 00:42:19.626
And family's really
what you got.

00:42:19.626 --> 00:42:23.751
And it made me and my wife a
lot closer, that's for sure.

00:42:23.751 --> 00:42:30.042
TERISA: I knew James is
really good under pressure.

00:42:30.042 --> 00:42:31.876
I just didn't ever
know how good.

00:42:31.876 --> 00:42:35.459
JAMES: She was stronger
than I thought she would be.
That's the truth.

00:42:35.459 --> 00:42:38.209
So it was a test. That
was a test on both sides.

00:42:38.209 --> 00:42:40.417
But she faired
well apparently.

00:42:40.417 --> 00:42:46.959
I mean we're both out.
You know we're both alive.
And so kudos I guess.

00:42:47.542 --> 00:42:51.083
NARRATOR: Ten days
after James gets the all
clear from his doctors,

00:42:51.083 --> 00:42:54.250
with some help from his
 sons, he is back out hunting.

00:42:54.250 --> 00:42:59.042
But this time he's better
prepared and much more
aware of the dangers.

00:42:59.042 --> 00:43:05.542
JAMES: My take away
on this, if you're gonna go
hunting in any area, swamp,

00:43:05.542 --> 00:43:07.542
make sure you know
what's in there.

00:43:07.542 --> 00:43:13.250
Don't always expect them to
be in the same spot or even
in the same environment.

00:43:13.250 --> 00:43:17.876
Come prepared for the
worst day of your life.

00:43:17.876 --> 00:43:19.792
That will save your life.

00:43:21.167 --> 00:43:27.334
OLIVIA: I think the whole
situation with the bees is
just a product of nature.

00:43:27.334 --> 00:43:35.000
We have to be adaptable
the same way that the bees
are trying to be adaptable.

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:38.042
And so they just happened
to stop on that pole.

00:43:38.042 --> 00:43:40.834
The guys just happened
to not see them.

00:43:40.834 --> 00:43:46.167
And disturbed them and so,
I don't blame the bees.

00:43:46.167 --> 00:43:51.417
I just felt really
sorry for the guys who
stumbled into them.

00:43:51.417 --> 00:43:54.501
And were stung relentlessly.

00:43:54.501 --> 00:43:57.834
But I'm also very grateful
that they survived

00:43:57.834 --> 00:44:01.375
and I'm grateful that
I could have offered

00:44:01.375 --> 00:44:06.042
whatever assistant that I was
able to on that day.

